.TH TUPLE 3 "2026" "y-- standard library" "y-- Value Types"
.SH NAME
tuple \- built-in tuple value type methods
.SH DESCRIPTION
Tuples in y-- are ordered, immutable sequences of elements. Their structure and contents
cannot be changed after creation, making them useful for fixed data collections or as dictionary keys.
.SH "CORE METHODS"
.TP
\fBcount(val)\fR -> int()
Returns the number of occurrences of \fBval\fR in the tuple.
.TP
\fBindex(val)\fR -> int()
Returns the first index of \fBval\fR, or -1 if it is not found.
.SH "MATH & AGGREGATION"
.TP
\fBsum() / min() / max() / average()\fR
Performs mathematical aggregations on the tuple. The \fBsum\fR and \fBaverage\fR methods
require all elements to be numeric. \fBmin\fR and \fBmax\fR can also compare strings.
.SH "UTILITIES"
.TP
\fBjoin([sep=""])\fR -> string()
Concatenates the string representations of all elements, separated by \fBsep\fR.
.TP
\fBsample(k)\fR -> tuple()
Returns a new tuple containing \fBk\fR randomly sampled distinct elements from the tuple.
.SH "FUNCTIONAL METHODS"
.TP
\fBAll_Of / Any_Of / None_Of / One_Of (lambda)\fR -> bool()
.TP
\fBselect / reject (lambda)\fR -> tuple()
Filters the tuple based on the lambda condition.
.TP
\fBmap(lambda)\fR -> tuple()
Returns a new tuple containing the mapped results.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
list(3)
